  {
    "id": "506cfbd36ca76011",
    "type": "comment",
    "z": "d7418a6b45fb8b58",
    "name": "State 4 - Cleanup. Use this state for teardown, acknowledgements, safe\\n shutdown, or resetting outputs before returning to READY. In some state\\n machine formalisms this work might be considered exit-state logic; in DFSM\\n it is often clearer to model it as an explicit state.",
    "info": "State 4 - Cleanup. Use this state for teardown, acknowledgements, safe\\n\nshutdown, or resetting outputs before returning to READY. In some state\\n\nmachine formalisms this work might be considered exit-state logic; in DFSM\\n\nit is often clearer to model it as an explicit state.",
    "x": 280,
    "y": 1080,
    "wires": []
  },
  {
    "id": "38cab3d05c6df7ca",
    "type": "comment",
    "z": "d7418a6b45fb8b58",
    "name": "Most DFSM transitions do not need a latch.\\n Use dfsm-util-latch only when a transition\\n request must be queued and later released or\\n canceled by a separate event.  Use clear only\\n when a queued transtion must be cancellable,\\n such as on reset or abort.",
    "info": "Most DFSM transitions do not need a latch.\\n\nUse dfsm-util-latch only when a transition\\n\nrequest must be queued and later released or\\n\ncanceled by a separate event.  Use clear only\\n\nwhen a queued transtion must be cancellable,\\n\nsuch as on reset or abort.",
    "x": 970,
    "y": 620,
    "wires": []
  },
